/* ===========================================================================
 * atlas-extra.js — circuits that are plotted but not measured.
 *
 * ★ THIS FILE IS HAND-MAINTAINED, AND IT IS THE ONLY DATA FILE THAT IS.
 *
 * `data/atlas.js` and `data/world.js` are GENERATED by trace/extract.py out of
 * Field Atlas 1.x and the traced geometry beside it. Nothing may be added to
 * either by hand — the next run of the script would delete it without a word.
 * That is fine for the 23 circuits 1.x actually carries, and it is a wall for
 * everything §03's atlas is eventually going to plot: a worldwide pin set has no
 * traced layout, no measured lap and no corner count, so there is nothing for the
 * extractor to extract and nowhere in its output for the rows to live.
 *
 * So they live here instead. This file is written by hand, is never generated
 * over, and holds the rows that are only a NAME AND A POSITION.
 *
 * ★ IT IS DELIBERATELY EMPTY.
 *
 * Theodor: "I mean worldwide, but don't add any more tracks because I will take
 * that into my own hands with you later."
 *
 * The section, the plotting, the labels, the filters, the search and the panel
 * routing are all finished and all take this array at whatever length it reaches.
 * What is not here is a few hundred circuits invented by a language model and
 * presented as his atlas — which is the same mistake Field Atlas 1.x made when it
 * seeded 29 suggested gear items nobody owned, and deleted its seed to escape.
 * See KIT_1X in js/gear.js for that story; it is the same rule twice.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE ROW SHAPE
 *
 *   id       string, unique across VENUES + TRACKS + this file. Kebab-case, no
 *            diacritics — it goes in a `circuit/<id>` panel route and in a URL
 *            hash. `spa`, `suzuka`, `mantorp`.
 *   name     what it is called, as it is written locally. Diacritics welcome.
 *   city     the nearest town, for the list's right-hand column.
 *   country  ISO 3166-1 alpha-2, uppercase. Used for grouping, nothing else.
 *   lat      degrees north, negative for south. Per CONVENTIONS §3.
 *   lon      degrees east, negative for west.
 *   kind     one of the disciplines the page already knows — 'karting', 'cup',
 *            'airshow'. It picks the filter chip and the pin's colour, and an
 *            unknown value simply falls back to karting rather than throwing.
 *
 * Example of one row, for when the day comes:
 *
 *   { id: 'mantorp', name: 'Mantorp Park', city: 'Mjölby', country: 'SE',
 *     lat: 58.3606, lon: 15.2864, kind: 'cup' },
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT THESE ROWS CANNOT DO, and why nothing here has to guard against it
 *
 * A row in this file has no `track`, so it has no lap length, no corner count and
 * no path. js/main.js therefore plots it, names it, searches it and flies the
 * camera to it — and does NOT give it a panel, because there would be nothing in
 * the panel. The list marks those rows `PLOTTED`, so the page never implies it
 * knows a circuit it has only located. Give a row real geometry one day and it
 * belongs in 1.x and in trace/extract.py, not here.
 * ======================================================================== */

/** @type {{id:string,name:string,city:string,country:string,lat:number,lon:number,kind:string}[]} */
export const EXTRA_TRACKS = [];
